%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6DAE4C68] # RLC Circuits
%[text] [⇦ DC Circuit Analysis Main Menu](file:../../MainMenu.m)
%[text] **Learning Goals**
%[text] - Compare the response of first and second order circuits.
%[text] - Analyze a second order RLC circuit differential equation.
%[text] - Solve for the natural frequency and damping in an RLC circuit.
%[text] - Compare underdamped, overdamped, and critically damped circuits. \
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_57CDE7EA] ## Background: RC and RL circuits
%[text] A capacitor behaves as an energy storage device. When a voltage source is applied, the capacitor charges. If the source is removed, the capacitor discharges. The time constant $\\tau = RC${"altText":"\tau = RC"} characterizes the charging and discharging rate in the series RC circuit. The behavior of the RC circuit is discussed in detail in [RCCircuits.m](<matlab:open(fullfile(currentProject().RootFolder,'Scripts','RCCircuits.m'))>).
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![RCCharging-Discharging.png](text:image:6af2)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Illustration of capacitor charging and discharging. Notice that the y-axis shows the voltage.*
%[text] An inductor also acts as an energy storage device – storing energy in an induced magnetic field. This is different from a capacitor, which stores energy in an electric field. The charging behavior of an inductor is analogous but distinct from that of a capacitor. When a *capacitor* charges, the voltage increases, and the current (which is initially large) diminishes. The opposite is true in an *inductor*: the current increases while the inductor charges until it reaches a maximum current (the current in the circuit if the inductor is short-circuited). Simultaneously, the voltage across the inductor drops exponentially from the supply voltage to zero. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![RLCharging-Discharging.png](text:image:56eb)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Illustration of an inductor charging and discharging. Notice that the y-axis now shows current.* 
%[text] ![interact.png](text:image:1d23) **Activity.** The behavior of switched RC and RL circuits are simulated in 
%[text]{"align":"center"} [![](text:image:1abc)](<matlab: RCRLCircuits>)
%[text]{"align":"center"} [`RCRLCircuits.slx`](<matlab: RCRLCircuits>)
%[text] Open the model and follow the instructions in the model canvas to complete the activity.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_67BB2C97] ## Introduction
%[text] Both RL and RC circuits display *first order* behavior, so-called because the circuits follow solutions to first order differential equations. The resulting solutions are exponential decay curves in both the charging and discharging circuits. Combine an inductor and capacitor into a single circuit, and it exhibits more complex *second order* behavior.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![RLC\_switch.png](text:image:49da)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *An RLC circuit with a switch*
%[text] ![interact.png](text:image:0474) **Activity.** The behavior of a switched RLC circuit is simulated in 
%[text]{"align":"center"} [![image3549.png](text:image:1ef2)](<matlab: RLCCircuit>)
%[text]{"align":"center"} [`RLCCircuit.slx`](<matlab: RLCCircuit>)
%[text] Open the model and follow the instructions in the model canvas to complete the activity.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BC046A5C] ## Discharging response
%[text] In this section, the differential equation for the discharging RLC circuit is derived and analyzed. The differential equation offers insight into the behavior of the RLC circuit. This section assumes that the circuit has been connected to the voltage source $V${"altText":"V"} for a long time and then switched to the discharging configuration.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![RLCDischarge.png](text:image:2d07)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Discharging RLC circuit*
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9F03327D] ### **Differential equation**
%[text] To derive the ODE, start by applying Kirchhoff's voltage law around the circuit:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $V\_L + V\_{R} +  V\_{C} = 0${"altText":"V\_L + V\_{R} +  V\_{C} = 0"}
%[text] Because the circuit is discharging, the source term is absent. Substitute the expressions for the inductor and resistor in terms of the current to find
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ L \\frac{\\text{d}i}{\\text{d}t} +i R +  V\_C = 0${"altText":" L \frac{\text{d}i}{\text{d}t} +i R +  V\_C = 0"}
%[text] The current is related to the voltage across the capacitor through
%[text]{"align":"center"} $i = C \\frac{\\text{d}V\_C}{\\text{d}t} ${"altText":"i = C \frac{\text{d}V\_C}{\text{d}t} "}
%[text] The current $i${"altText":"i"} flows through each element, so the ODE can be rewritten as
%[text]{"align":"center"} $LC \\frac{\\text{d}^2 V\_C}{\\text{d}t^2} + RC \\frac{\\text{d}V\_C}{\\text{d}t} + V\_C= 0${"altText":"LC \frac{\text{d}^2 V\_C}{\text{d}t^2} + RC \frac{\text{d}V\_C}{\text{d}t} + V\_C= 0"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_416EF8A5] ### Analysis
%[text] **General form of the ODE**
%[text] This type of second order ODE is commonly written in a general form:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{\\text{d}^2 x}{\\text{d}t^2} + 2 \\alpha \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} + {\\omega\_0}^2 x = 0${"altText":"\frac{\text{d}^2 x}{\text{d}t^2} + 2 \alpha \frac{\text{d}x}{\text{d}t} + {\omega\_0}^2 x = 0"}
%[text] Rewriting the RC circuit ODE as
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{\\text{d}^2 V\_C}{\\text{d}t^2} + 2\\frac{R}{2L} \\frac{\\text{d}V\_C}{\\text{d}t} + \\frac{1}{LC} V\_C= 0${"altText":"\frac{\text{d}^2 V\_C}{\text{d}t^2} + 2\frac{R}{2L} \frac{\text{d}V\_C}{\text{d}t} + \frac{1}{LC} V\_C= 0"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C4068D02] implies the constants are
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\alpha = \\frac{R}{2L}${"altText":"\alpha = \frac{R}{2L}"}  and  $\\omega\_0 = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{LC}}${"altText":"\omega\_0 = \frac{1}{\sqrt{LC}}"} 
%[text] with the general form
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{\\text{d}^2 V\_C}{\\text{d}t^2} + 2 \\alpha \\frac{\\text{d}V\_C}{\\text{d}t} + {\\omega\_0}^2 V\_C= 0${"altText":"\frac{\text{d}^2 V\_C}{\text{d}t^2} + 2 \alpha \frac{\text{d}V\_C}{\text{d}t} + {\omega\_0}^2 V\_C= 0"}
%[text] This general form simplifies the analysis below.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_24181ACA] **Solution**
%[text] To solve the ODE, assume a solution of the form
%[text]{"align":"center"} $V\_C(t) = \\text{e}^{s t}${"altText":"V\_C(t) = \text{e}^{s t}"}
%[text] Substitute this expression into the ODE to obtain
%[text]{"align":"center"} $s^2 \\text{e}^{st} + 2 \\alpha s \\text{e}^{st} + {\\omega\_0}^2 \\text{e}^\_{st} = 0${"altText":"s^2 \text{e}^{st} + 2 \alpha s \text{e}^{st} + {\omega\_0}^2 \text{e}^\_{st} = 0"}
%[text] Factoring the expression yields
%[text]{"align":"center"} $(s^2 + 2 \\alpha s + {\\omega\_0}^2) \\cdot \\text{e}^{st} = 0${"altText":"(s^2 + 2 \alpha s + {\omega\_0}^2) \cdot \text{e}^{st} = 0"}
%[text] Because the exponential is nonzero, the quadratic must be zero:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $s^2  + 2\\alpha s + {\\omega\_0}^2 = 0${"altText":"s^2  + 2\alpha s + {\omega\_0}^2 = 0"}
%[text] This equation is commonly referred to as the *characteristic equation*. The roots of the characteristic equation determine the behavior of the ODE solution. Using the quadratic formula shows that the solutions are
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\begin{array}{rl}\ns\_{1} = - \\alpha + \\sqrt{ \\alpha^2 - {\\omega\_0}^2 }\n\\\\\ns\_{2} = - \\alpha - \\sqrt{ \\alpha^2 - {\\omega\_0}^2 }\n\\end{array}${"altText":"\begin{array}{rl} s\_{1} = - \alpha + \sqrt{ \alpha^2 - {\omega\_0}^2 } \\ s\_{2} = - \alpha - \sqrt{ \alpha^2 - {\omega\_0}^2 } \end{array}"}
%[text] Because both of the characteristic roots satisfy the ODE, the solution is a linear combination of both:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $V\_c(t) = A\_1 \\text{e}^{s\_{1} t} + A\_2 \\text{e}^{s\_2  t}${"altText":"V\_c(t) = A\_1 \text{e}^{s\_{1} t} + A\_2 \text{e}^{s\_2  t}"}
%[text] Since the capacitor has been charged for a long time, the initial voltage is $V\_C(0) = V${"altText":"V\_C(0) = V"}. Because the equation is second order, another initial condition is required. Assume that the voltage is not changing when the simulation starts: ${V\_C}'(0) = 0${"altText":"{V\_C}'(0) = 0"}. Using these two initial conditions, you can solve for the coefficients $A\_1${"altText":"A\_1"} and $A\_2${"altText":"A\_2"}. The resulting solution is illustrated below.
%[text] ![interact.png](text:image:9fc2) **Activity.** The code below solves for the coefficients $A\_i${"altText":"A\_i"} and plots the solution for the discharging RLC circuit.
%[text] - Adjust the sliders so that the characteristic roots $s${"altText":"s"} have only a real component. What kind of behavior is observed?
%[text] - What parameter values yield characteristic roots with an imaginary component? Refer to the expression for the roots of the characteristic equation, $s${"altText":"s"}.
%[text] - Adjust the parameters below so that the roots have an imaginary component. How does the behavior change? \
% Set the parameters
R = 10; % Ohms %[control:slider:3685]{"position":[5,7]}
L = 0.0005; % Henries %[control:slider:4bed]{"position":[5,11]}
C = 0.000021; % Farads %[control:slider:3f9d]{"position":[5,13]}
V = 12; % Volts %[control:slider:0a86]{"position":[5,7]}

% Compute the characteristic roots
alpha = R/(2*L);
omega0 = 1/sqrt(L*C);
s1 = -alpha + sqrt(alpha^2 - omega0^2);
s2 = -alpha - sqrt(alpha^2 - omega0^2);

% Compute the coefficients and solution
A2 = V/(1 - s2/s1);
A1 = V - A2;
t = linspace(0,0.001,1e3);
Vc = A1*exp(s1*t) + A2*exp(s2*t);

% This code plots the result
figure("Position",[0 0 700 400])
plot(t,real(Vc),"LineWidth",1.5)
xlabel("t (s)")
ylabel("V_c (V)")
title("Discharging RLC with s_1 = " + num2str(s1,"%0.3g") + " and s_2 = " + num2str(s2,"%0.3g"),"FontWeight","normal")
axis([0 0.001 -10 25])
%%
%[text] **Charging circuit**
%[text] The transient response of the *charging* circuit has the same form as that of the discharging circuit. The characteristic roots are identical, so the responses follow the same patterns. However, the solutions to the charging circuit are distinct from the discharging solutions due to the voltage source and initial conditions.
%[text] ![write.png](text:image:8810) **Exercise.** Derive the ODE for the *charging* circuit and solve for its characteristic roots and solutions. Plot the solutions and compare them to those of the discharging circuit.
% Write code to plot the solutions here

