%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B53AEE63] ## Switching circuit
%[text] Manual switches are helpful for building intuition about how RC, RL, and RLC circuits work. In practice, however, the RLC circuit charging and discharging often occur in sequence in the same circuit, without a manual switch. Regular switching is generated by another circuit, typically constructed using transistors. Modeling the details of the switching elements in the circuit is not necessary for analyzing the response. 
%[text] An easy approach to modeling a switched input is to use a variable voltage source. For example, a square wave source produces the same behavior as the manual switch but at regular intervals. A variable source will be used in the remainder of this lesson, rather than a manual switch. This allows both the charging and discharging characteristics of the circuit to be viewed within the same graph.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![RLC\_SquareWaveResponse.png](text:image:184a)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *RLC circuit with a square wave input and its response.*
%[text] ![interact.png](text:image:8793) **Activity.** A signal generator and oscilloscope can be used to analyze an RLC circuit with a switched input. A virtual signal generator and oscilloscope are simulated in:
%[text]{"align":"center"} [![image5554.png](text:image:5c6d)](<matlab: VirtualOscilloscope>)
%[text]{"align":"center"} [`VirtualOscilloscope.slx`](<matlab: VirtualOscilloscope>)
%[text] **Note:** Watch [this 1-minute instructional video](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/81383420/145592175-ed68b140-f7d7-4355-85d4-a47feb8a82ec.mp4) to learn how to use the virtual oscilloscope. 
%[text] Open the model and then perform the following to complete the activity. 
%[text]     1\. Connect the series RLC circuit as shown in the diagram below. Use $R = 75${"altText":"R = 75"} $\\Omega${"altText":"\Omega"}, $L = 15${"altText":"L = 15"} mH, and $C = 1${"altText":"C = 1"} µF. Ensure that the source is connected to the source generator.
%[text]     2\. Connect the leads of the oscilloscope to measure the voltage across the capacitor. Then, run the model.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![lab\_model.png](text:image:2b91)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Diagram of completed step 2: the connected circuit*
%[text]     3\. Turn on the signal generator.
%[text]     4\. Set the input waveform to the square wave using the dial.
%[text]     5\. Set the input frequency to 200 Hz.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![SignalGen.png](text:image:34c4)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Signal generator with the interactions for steps 3-5 highlighted.*
%[text]     6\. Turn on the oscilloscope.
%[text]     7\. Set the oscilloscope **Display Mode** to **Triggered** and the **Trigger cycles** to **3**.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![oscilloscope\_labels.png](text:image:6168)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Oscilloscope with interactions for steps 6 and 7 highlighted.*
%[text]     7\. If correct, you should observe the result shown below in the Oscilloscope plot. Notice that both charging and discharging responses are visible. Based on the observed output, are the characteristic roots of this circuit real or complex?
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:6a3f)
