%[text] %[text:anchor:H_DC7B7190] ## Response classification
%[text] The transient response of the RLC circuit is characterized by two parameters:
%[text] 1. $\\zeta = \\frac{\\alpha}{\\omega\_0}${"altText":"\zeta = \frac{\alpha}{\omega\_0}"}, the *damping factor*.
%[text] 2. $\\omega\_0${"altText":"\omega\_0"}, the *undamped natural frequency* or *resonant frequency*. \
%[text] Recall that these parameters are defined through
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\alpha = \\frac{R}{2L}${"altText":"\alpha = \frac{R}{2L}"}  and  $\\omega\_0 = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{LC}}${"altText":"\omega\_0 = \frac{1}{\sqrt{LC}}"} 
%[text] The damping factor, $\\zeta${"altText":"\zeta"}, is commonly used to classify the system. Solutions fall into three basic classifications: *overdamped*, *critically damped*, and *underdamped*.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4C28FC62] ### Overdamped
%[text] The RLC circuit is overdamped when the solutions are devoid of oscillatory behavior (the oscillations are completely "damped out"). Recall that for a complex exponential:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\text{e}^{(a + bi)} = \\text{e}^{a} ( \\cos b+  i \\sin b)${"altText":"\text{e}^{(a + bi)} = \text{e}^{a} ( \cos b+  i \sin b)"}
%[text] This implies that, if the exponent is strictly real, the solution will be purely exponential: $\\text{e}^a${"altText":"\text{e}^a"}. In this case, there will be no oscillatory behavior. In terms of the RLC circuit characteristic equation, this happens when the characteristic roots have only a real component. Recall that the characteristic roots are
%[text]{"align":"center"} $s\_{\\pm} = - \\alpha \\pm \\sqrt{ \\alpha^2 - {\\omega\_0}^2 }${"altText":"s\_{\pm} = - \alpha \pm \sqrt{ \alpha^2 - {\omega\_0}^2 }"}
%[text] The roots are real if $\\alpha^2 - {\\omega\_0}^2 \\geq 0${"altText":"\alpha^2 - {\omega\_0}^2 \geq 0"}.The system is overdamped when $\\alpha^2 - \\omega\_0^2 \> 0${"altText":"\alpha^2 - \omega\_0^2 \> 0"}. Notice that this definition excludes the edge case where $\\alpha^2 - {\\omega\_0}^2 = 0${"altText":"\alpha^2 - {\omega\_0}^2 = 0"}. In terms of the damping factor, $\\zeta = \\alpha / \\omega\_0${"altText":"\zeta = \alpha \/ \omega\_0"}, the system is overdamped when $\\zeta \> 1${"altText":"\zeta \> 1"}.
%[text] ![interact.png](text:image:4392) **Activity.** Assume that a 10 µF capacitor and a 5 mH inductor are used in the series RLC circuit analyzed in the previous sections.
%[text] - What resistor values will produce an overdamped response? 
%[text] - What resistor value produces a damping factor $\\zeta = 1.5${"altText":"\zeta = 1.5"}?
%[text] - If you have not already done so, construct a series RLC circuit and connect it as shown in the previous section. Adjust the circuit element values and measure the response of the circuit with a damping factor of 1.5 using the virtual oscilloscope: \
%[text]{"align":"center"} [![image5554.png](text:image:7e30)](<matlab: VirtualOscilloscope>)
%[text]{"align":"center"} [`VirtualOscilloscope.slx`](<matlab: VirtualOscilloscope>)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A4509D40] ### Critically damped
%[text] The RLC circuit is critically damped when the oscillations are removed with minimal damping. In terms of the characteristic roots, $s = - \\alpha \\pm \\sqrt{ \\alpha^2 - {\\omega\_0}^2 }${"altText":"s = - \alpha \pm \sqrt{ \alpha^2 - {\omega\_0}^2 }"}, this occurs when 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\alpha^2 - {\\omega\_0}^2 = 0${"altText":"\alpha^2 - {\omega\_0}^2 = 0"}
%[text] In terms of the damping factor, the system is critically damped when $\\zeta = 1${"altText":"\zeta = 1"}.
%[text] ![interact.png](text:image:1b4b) **Activity.** Assume that a 10 µF capacitor and a 5 mH inductor are used.
%[text] - What resistor values will produce a critically damped response? 
%[text] - What resistor value produces a damping factor $\\zeta = 1${"altText":"\zeta = 1"}?
%[text] - Adjust the circuit element values and measure the response of the circuit with a damping factor of 1 using the virtual oscilloscope, [`VirtualOscilloscope.slx`](<matlab: VirtualOscilloscope>).
%[text] - How does the critically damped response compare to the overdamped response you measured in the previous activity?
%[text] - Are there any oscillations present? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_26086E70] ### Underdamped
%[text] The RLC circuit is underdamped when oscillatory behavior is present in the response. In terms of the characteristic roots, $s = - \\alpha \\pm \\sqrt{ \\alpha^2 - {\\omega\_0}^2 }${"altText":"s = - \alpha \pm \sqrt{ \alpha^2 - {\omega\_0}^2 }"}, this occurs when 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\alpha^2 - {\\omega\_0}^2 \< 0${"altText":"\alpha^2 - {\omega\_0}^2 \< 0"}
%[text] The resulting complex roots imply nonzero sine and cosine factors in the complex exponential. In terms of the damping factor, the system is underdamped when $\\zeta \< 1${"altText":"\zeta \< 1"}.
%[text] ![interact.png](text:image:2edb) **Activity.** Assume that a 10 µF capacitor and a 5 mH inductor are used.
%[text] - What resistor values will produce an underdamped response? 
%[text] - What resistor value produces a damping factor $\\zeta = 0.5${"altText":"\zeta = 0.5"}?
%[text] - Adjust the circuit element values and measure the circuit's response with a damping factor of 0.5 using the virtual oscilloscope, [`VirtualOscilloscope.slx`](<matlab: VirtualOscilloscope>).
%[text] - How does the underdamped response compare to the overdamped response you measured in the previous activity?
%[text] - Are there any oscillations present? \
